### What are intermediate operations?

All intermediate operations are lazy. They return a new Stream and execute only when a terminal operation triggers the pipeline.

### How does filter work?

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.math.BigDecimal;
import java.util.List;

public class FilterDemo {
    record Account(String id, BigDecimal balance, boolean active) {}

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        List<Account> accounts = List.of(
            new Account("SAV2001", new BigDecimal("125000"), true),
            new Account("SAV2002", new BigDecimal("8000"), false),
            new Account("CUR3001", new BigDecimal("250000"), true)
        );

        // filter: keep active accounts
        accounts.stream()
            .filter(Account::active)
            .forEach(a -> System.out.println(a.id() + ": Rs." + a.balance()));
    }
}
```

```text
SAV2001: Rs.125000
CUR3001: Rs.250000
```

### How does map work?

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.math.BigDecimal;
import java.util.List;

public class MapDemo {
    record Account(String id, BigDecimal balance) {}

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        List<Account> accounts = List.of(
            new Account("SAV2001", new BigDecimal("125000")),
            new Account("CUR3001", new BigDecimal("250000"))
        );

        // map: Account -> String (extract formatted balance)
        List<String> summaries = accounts.stream()
            .map(a -> a.id() + ": Rs." + a.balance())
            .toList();

        System.out.println(summaries);
    }
}
```

```text
[SAV2001: Rs.125000, CUR3001: Rs.250000]
```

### How does flatMap work?

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.util.List;

public class FlatMapDemo {
    record Customer(String name, List<String> accountIds) {}

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        List<Customer> customers = List.of(
            new Customer("Rajesh", List.of("SAV2001", "CUR3001")),
            new Customer("Priya", List.of("SAV2002")),
            new Customer("Amit", List.of("SAV2003", "FD4001", "CUR3002"))
        );

        // Without flatMap: Stream<List<String>> (nested!)
        // With flatMap: Stream<String> (flat!)
        List<String> allAccounts = customers.stream()
            .flatMap(c -> c.accountIds().stream())
            .toList();

        System.out.println(allAccounts);
    }
}
```

```text
[SAV2001, CUR3001, SAV2002, SAV2003, FD4001, CUR3002]
```

### How are stateful operations different from stateless?

| Operation | Stateful? | Memory | Safe on infinite stream? |
|-----------|-----------|--------|-------------------------|
| filter | No | O(1) | Yes |
| map | No | O(1) | Yes |
| flatMap | No | O(1) per element | Yes |
| peek | No | O(1) | Yes |
| sorted | Yes | O(n) | No: hangs or OOM |
| distinct | Yes | O(n) | Technically yes, but grows forever |
| limit | Yes | O(1) counter | Yes: short-circuits |
| skip | Yes | O(1) counter | Yes |

### What is the performance tip for operation ordering?

```java
package com.bank.core;

import java.util.List;
import java.util.Comparator;
import java.math.BigDecimal;

public class OrderingTip {
    record Account(String id, BigDecimal balance, boolean active) {}

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        List<Account> accounts = List.of(
            new Account("SAV2001", new BigDecimal("125000"), true),
            new Account("SAV2002", new BigDecimal("8000"), false),
            new Account("CUR3001", new BigDecimal("250000"), true),
            new Account("SAV2003", new BigDecimal("50000"), false),
            new Account("CUR3002", new BigDecimal("180000"), true)
        );

        // WRONG: sort ALL 5, THEN filter to 3
        // Sorts 5 elements unnecessarily

        // CORRECT: filter FIRST to 3, THEN sort 3
        List<Account> result = accounts.stream()
            .filter(Account::active)                                    // reduces to 3
            .sorted(Comparator.comparing(Account::balance).reversed())  // sorts only 3
            .toList();

        result.forEach(a -> System.out.println(a.id() + ": Rs." + a.balance()));
    }
}
```

```text
CUR3001: Rs.250000
CUR3002: Rs.180000
SAV2001: Rs.125000
```

### What about peek for debugging?

peek accepts a Consumer and observes each element without modifying the stream. It is designed for debugging only. Do not use it for business logic because the JVM can optimise away peek calls and parallel streams execute peek in unpredictable order.

### What should a senior engineer know?

The practical distinction between stateless and stateful matters for performance and correctness. Place filter before sorted to reduce the buffer size. Never use sorted on infinite streams. Use limit after filter when you need a bounded result from a potentially large source. Understanding these categories helps optimise pipeline ordering.
